  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Education &amp; empowerment through community-run Play and Reading Schemes (Ghana)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Working in rural villages in Northern Ghana Lively Minds follows an integrated approach to improving learning and health for young children. Working with volunteers drawn from the most disadvantaged women in the communities they work with, they establish &#x2018;Play Schemes&apos; that encourage early childhood learning for children aged 3-5. Through these schemes, the cognitive development of these children is supported in a measurable way, while growth in the status and self-esteem of the volunteers is also supported, with a view not only to their place in the programme, but also in their family and community life. Play Schemes are followed by &#x2018;Reading Schemes&apos;, through which older children volunteers support reading practice for 5-8 year olds, thus improving reading skills for both groups of children. These Schemes help children develop essential skills - such as reasoning, numeracy and literacy - at a young age when this kind of creative education makes a big difference to future development. Across both schemes, there is a complementary focus on hygiene practices, which contribute to improving the health of young children and the community as a whole. Over the project lifespan, outcomes will include improved cognitive skills for 4500 children aged 3-5; increased language and comprehension skills for 4500 children aged 5-8 and 1500 children aged 11-13; improved school attainment for participants of Play and Reading Schemes; improved confidence and wellbeing of 900 Play Scheme Volunteers; and increased hand washing in project participants leading to reduction in diarrheal disease.</narrative>
  </description>
